=== task: mcq medium
title: Ten largest from millions
prompt: A sensor sends millions of readings, one at a time. A program must keep the 10 largest seen so far, using as little memory as possible. Which structure fits?
* A min-heap holding 10 readings :: Right. Its root is the smallest of the 10, the one a bigger reading replaces: O(log 10) per reading and only 10 readings stored.
- A max-heap of every reading so far :: That works, but it stores millions of readings.
- A max-heap holding 10 readings :: Its root would be the largest of the 10, but the one to throw out is the smallest.
- A sorted list of every reading so far :: Inserting into a sorted list shifts items: O(n) per reading, and millions stored.
hint: Which reading should a bigger newcomer push out?
hint: Which kind of heap keeps that reading at the root?
right: A min-heap of size 10: the weakest of the top 10 sits at the root, so checking and replacing it is fast, and memory stays at 10 readings.
wrong: To keep the 10 largest, you need quick access to the smallest of them, the one to evict. That's the root of a min-heap of size 10.

